{
    // TYPE CHECKING, WITHOUT TYPESCRIPT.
    //
    // Nothing here is compiled. `noEmit` is on, there is no build step, and not
    // one `.ts` file exists in the product. What this buys is `checkJs`:
    // TypeScript reads the plain JavaScript and the JSDoc that is already in it
    // and reports the errors a type checker can see. `npm run typecheck` runs
    // it; `npm run check` runs it beside the linter.
    //
    // ── THE BOUNDARY, AND WHY IT IS WHERE IT IS ────────────────────────────
    //
    // `include` below is FOUR trees, not the whole game. That is deliberate and
    // it is the honest version of this feature.
    //
    // Checking all of `src/game/` reports ~250 errors. Almost none of them are
    // defects — they are JSDoc `@param {{...}}` blocks that describe an options
    // object less completely than the code uses it, which is a documentation
    // debt worth paying but not one worth paying in a single 250-site commit
    // nobody can review. Turning them all green by pasting `any` everywhere
    // would produce a checker that passes and proves nothing, which is worse
    // than no checker.
    //
    // So the checked set is the four trees where this project's real bugs have
    // actually lived, per its own incident record in `REVIEW.md` and
    // `HANDOFF.md`:
    //
    //   kernel/    the save schema, progress, health, inventory, run mode.
    //              A wrong shape here corrupts a save.
    //   world/     the room graph, key economy, blockers, doors, puzzles.
    //              Every softlock this game has ever had lived in this tree.
    //   combat/    the single damage entry point and the armour predicate.
    //   physics/   the body that decides where the player may stand.
    //
    // Everything outside them is UNCHECKED. Presentation code (`fx/`, `ui/`,
    // `render/`, `assets/`), the boss roster, `index.js`, `player.js`,
    // `enemy.js` and the fourteen level definitions get no type analysis at
    // all. `REVIEW.md` says so in the same words, because a reader who assumes
    // "the repo has a type checker" means "the repo is type-checked" would be
    // wrong by about 80%.
    //
    // The frozen My-Engine trees (`src/engine/`, `src/voxel/`, `src/combat/`,
    // `src/characters/`, `src/audio/`) are outside the boundary on purpose:
    // repository policy is that they are not edited from here, and a checker
    // that reports errors nobody is allowed to fix is a checker people learn to
    // ignore. They are still PULLED IN as dependencies of the checked trees, so
    // a checked file calling into the kit is still checked against the kit's
    // real signatures — `skipLibCheck` and the absence of `checkJs` errors
    // outside `include` is what keeps their own internals quiet.
    //
    // ── EXPANDING IT ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    //
    // Add a tree to `include`, run `npm run typecheck`, fix what it says. The
    // fix is nearly always widening a JSDoc `@param` to match what the function
    // is really passed. Do NOT add `// @ts-ignore` to close the gap: an
    // ignored error is a lie with a comment on it, and the whole point of this
    // file is that the repository is harder to lie to.
    "compilerOptions": {
        "target": "ES2022",
        "module": "ESNext",
        "moduleResolution": "bundler",
        "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],

        "allowJs": true,
        "noEmit": true,

        // FALSE, and the boundary is per-file instead: every file in the four
        // trees below carries `// @ts-check` on line 1.
        //
        // `checkJs: true` is not a boundary. TypeScript pulls in everything the
        // included files import — the frozen kit, `assets/`, `characters/`,
        // `fx/` — and checks all of it, so `include` narrows what is NAMED and
        // nothing about what is REPORTED. The per-file pragma is the only way
        // to say "these files are checked and their dependencies are not".
        //
        // The obvious hole is that the pragma is one line anybody can delete,
        // so `tests/game/typecheck-boundary.spec.mjs` reads this file, walks
        // those trees, and fails if any file has lost it — which also makes
        // "add a file to a checked tree" a thing the suite notices.
        "checkJs": false,

        // Off on purpose. `strict` turns on `strictNullChecks`, which in a
        // codebase written without it reports every `foo?.bar` chain the author
        // already guarded by hand. The errors that survive with it off are the
        // ones that are wrong in any dialect: a property that does not exist, a
        // call with the wrong arity, a value of the wrong kind. Those are what
        // this gate is for. Tightening it is a later, separate decision.
        "strict": false,

        // Vendored three.js under `lib/` is a minified build with its own
        // hand-written .d.ts; checking that is someone else's job.
        "skipLibCheck": true,

        // No ambient @types packages. The only ambient declaration this project
        // needs is its own, below.
        "types": [],

        "resolveJsonModule": true,
        "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true
    },
    "include": [
        "types/**/*.d.ts",
        "src/game/kernel/**/*.js",
        "src/game/world/**/*.js",
        "src/game/combat/**/*.js",
        "src/game/physics/**/*.js"
    ]
}
